{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/full-line-stripe-analysis.md", "content": "# Full-line stripe detection and output validation

When demos of mixed geometry may have a shared rule, inventory full-width uniform rows and full-height uniform columns independently before projecting anything. A task may color complete structural lines while preserving every non-line cell, including noise or markers near the line. Distinguish genuinely uniform lines from near-uniform rows/columns with scattered markers; a near-stripe can be negative evidence against simple 'mostly uniform' selection.

Record deltas for every cell on candidate lines: demos can indicate either recoloring only the original stripe color or overwriting the entire line, so don't assume one. Compare horizontal-only, vertical-only, and both-orientation rules against every demo and preserved off-line markers. Apply only the rule that explains all examples, then mechanically validate output dimensions and row widths. This is a discriminating checklist, not a solved transformation for any specific task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/full-line-stripe-analysis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/periodic-row-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic row-sequence demonstrations

When a demo contains a compact non-background motif and the test contains full-width rows of single colors, consider whether the task is sequence extrapolation across rows rather than a cell-local transformation. Read the input/output row-by-row, identify unchanged rows and inserted/recolored rows, and infer any cycle only after checking the complete output. Apply the resulting sequence to the test's row structure and verify every row, including the cycle boundary; do not assume original test rows are all preserved as markers.

For the reviewed example, the 5x5 input rows were `1,9,7,0,0`; `9,1,9,7,0`; `0,9,1,9,7`; `0,0,9,1,9`; `0,0,0,9,1`. The output continued/filled the row-wise periodic pattern so each row became a repeated sequence of colors, with row starts cycling `1,9,7,9,1`. This is a clue to test row-cycle extrapolation, not sufficient alone to assert a unique rule for a different test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/periodic-row-sequence.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/output-shape-audit.md", "content": "# Output shape audit after invalid-grid feedback

When demos show different output and input dimensions, establish the output geometry separately from the cell-content rule. Never presume that output preserves input dimensions or infer the shape from a salient portion of the test. Record each pair's exact input/output dimensions and test the proposed structural mapping against all examples.

Before submission, mechanically check row count, each row's width, and legal cell values. If evaluator feedback says the grid was not a valid ARC grid, treat that first as a dimension/serialization failure—not semantic evidence—and repair the structural output shape before another submission. In strict action tasks, make this audit before emitting the JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/output-shape-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous object-cleaning demos: session note

When several differently sized grids contain solid monochrome rectangles among sparse alien-colored marks, don't infer a simple global recoloring from palette overlap. Compare each pair's deltas and classify objects: the demos can show preservation of intact rectangles while isolated noise is erased and intrusions inside a target object are repaired. Record separately (a) isolated marks cleared to the background, (b) interior foreign-color cells replaced by the enclosing object's color, (c) genuine missing cells filled to complete a rectangle, and (d) non-target/background regions left unchanged. Do not automatically fill every connected component's bounding box: validate each proposed target's original geometry and its output mask. The common operation may be shape- or context-selected rather than applying to every colored cell.

After a wrong answer, reject the entire selection/repair hypothesis and replay all demos; changing only the set of speckles or rectangle bounds is not sufficient. A new paid example is worthwhile only when surviving selection rules predict different test outputs. Mechanically generate and validate the output grid before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-structured-motif-reassessment", "file": "references/session-285c935d-review.md", "content": "# Session review: stripes versus a sparse diagonal test motif

The stream supplied three demonstrations. In the 8×8 pair, rows of color 3 remained, rows 2 and 6 became 7, while rows with colors 8 and 7 were retained. The 15×15 pair replaced zero rows with 4 except where some rows instead showed a repeated 5,3,7,2 sequence; existing stripe colors mostly remained. The 4×4 pair changed only the first row from 0 to 9, leaving 4,2,9 rows unchanged. These examples do not support a single simple diagonal-period fill, and the third demo contradicts treating every zero cell/row uniformly. The submitted periodic diagonal projection was incorrect.

Reusable review lesson: after a wrong projection, do not preserve the assumed period/orientation and make a local adjustment. Reconstruct all demonstrations and distinguish a row/sequence transformation from a rule conditioned on stripe arrangement or marker geometry. In particular, a structured test with diagonal tracks must be analyzed independently at coordinate level; do not assign colors by diagonal phase unless that exact relation explains every demo and preserves/recolors markers consistently. If remaining candidates differ on the test, use another demo only if it can discriminate them; otherwise choose a fully evidenced prediction rather than a speculative pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-structured-motif-reassessment/references/session-285c935d-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/large-output-analysis.md", "content": "# Large-output analysis

When output dimensions greatly exceed input dimensions, establish the transformation structurally rather than assuming a conventional same-size edit. First test whether the output consists of exact input tiles. Otherwise, check whether the input is preserved as a corner block and compare every added region against the input and against other added regions for a reproducible generation rule. Validate the whole output, including all added cells; extensive additions must not be dismissed as noise. A single example may establish dimensions without establishing how the additions are generated. Request another demo only when stated competing constructions predict different test outputs and the added example can discriminate between them.
